        <dc:subject>SofA motor movement</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Intentional control of attention: action planning primes action-related stimulus dimensions</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2007-1-11</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Intentional control of attention</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00426-005-0033-3</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-10 18:24:14</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://www.springer.com/tdm</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>22-29</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:0340-0727,%201430-2772">
        <prism:volume>71</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Psychological Research</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1007/s00426-005-0033-3</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>1</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Psychological Research</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0340-0727, 1430-2772</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_516">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;not read; used in discussion for RT;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What they tested&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They investigated whether &lt;strong&gt;planning an action influences attention and perception&lt;/strong&gt;, even before the action is actually executed. Specifically, participants prepared for either a &lt;strong&gt;reaching action&lt;/strong&gt; or a &lt;strong&gt;grasping action&lt;/strong&gt;. While they kept that action plan active (before doing it), they were presented with &lt;strong&gt;stimulus events&lt;/strong&gt; that varied in &lt;strong&gt;perceptual features&lt;/strong&gt; (like location or size). The question was:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Does preparing to move in a particular way make the brain process certain stimulus dimensions more effectively?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What they found&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
While participants were preparing a &lt;strong&gt;reaching action&lt;/strong&gt;, they detected changes in &lt;strong&gt;location-based stimuli&lt;/strong&gt; more quickly.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
When preparing a &lt;strong&gt;grasping action&lt;/strong&gt;, they detected changes in &lt;strong&gt;size-based stimuli&lt;/strong&gt; more quickly.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This effect happened &lt;strong&gt;before actual movement execution&lt;/strong&gt;, suggesting that merely &lt;strong&gt;planning the action biased perception&lt;/strong&gt; toward action-related perceptual dimensions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What it means (in simple terms)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Planning an action doesn’t just prepare the muscles — it also changes how your brain &lt;strong&gt;allocates attention&lt;/strong&gt; to the world. If you plan to reach for something, you become better at noticing where things are. If you plan to grasp something, you become better at noticing &lt;em&gt;what size&lt;/em&gt; things are. This suggests that &lt;strong&gt;action plans and attention/perception share processing resources&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it matters for your work&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Although this study is &lt;strong&gt;not about sense of agency per se&lt;/strong&gt;, it shows a key principle that is relevant for agency research:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Action preparation influences cognitive and perceptual processes even before action execution.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This supports the idea that in your experiment, &lt;strong&gt;active movement&lt;/strong&gt; (even just planning it) can change how participants process subsequent stimuli and make judgments — including &lt;em&gt;how they experience agency&lt;/em&gt;. This fits well with theoretical frameworks like the &lt;strong&gt;Theory of Event Coding (TEC)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;intentional weighting mechanisms&lt;/strong&gt;, which argue that action and perception are tightly coupled at early stages of processing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
    <bib:Article rdf:about="http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fpsyg.2011.00218/abstract">
        <z:itemType>journalArticle</z:itemType>
        <dcterms:isPartOf>
            <bib:Journal>
                <prism:volume>2</prism:volume>
                <dc:title>Frontiers in Psychology</dc:title>
                <dc:identifier>DOI 10.3389/fpsyg.2011.00218</dc:identifier>
                <dcterms:alternative>Front. Psychology</dcterms:alternative>
                <dc:identifier>ISSN 1664-1078</dc:identifier>
            </bib:Journal>
        </dcterms:isPartOf>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Brown</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Harriet</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Friston</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Karl</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Bestmann</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Sven</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
