        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_519"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_518"/>
        <dc:subject>SofA motor movement</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Active Inference, Attention, and Motor Preparation</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2011</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fpsyg.2011.00218/abstract</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-10 18:26:59</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_519">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;not red; used in discussion of RT; &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What they tested:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;The study examined how &lt;strong&gt;motor preparation and attention are linked&lt;/strong&gt; when planning and executing movements. They used adapted versions of a reaction-time paradigm (similar to a Posner cueing task) to see whether cues about a forthcoming movement could speed up reaction times and how &lt;strong&gt;attention in motor preparation&lt;/strong&gt; interacts with prediction of sensory consequences of action. The framework they tested is based on &lt;strong&gt;active inference and predictive coding&lt;/strong&gt;, which proposes that motor control and attention share mechanisms for predicting and preparing movements.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How it worked:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Participants were given cues about:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
what type of movement to prepare
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
which hand to use&lt;br&gt;The researchers then measured &lt;strong&gt;reaction times&lt;/strong&gt; to these movements under different cueing conditions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Main results (RT findings):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They found that &lt;strong&gt;valid cues about the movement and effector (hand)&lt;/strong&gt; led to &lt;strong&gt;faster RTs&lt;/strong&gt; than invalid cues.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
There was a significant interaction between the type of cue and movement execution: valid preparation sped responses more than invalid preparation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This suggests that &lt;strong&gt;attention and prediction in motor preparation improve RTs&lt;/strong&gt;, similar to how visual attention speeds perception in classic Posner tasks.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conceptual conclusion:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;These findings support the idea that &lt;strong&gt;motor planning and attention are deeply intertwined&lt;/strong&gt;, and that preparing a movement (i.e., planning a voluntary action) is not just about issuing a command — it also mobilizes attentional systems that &lt;em&gt;reduce reaction times&lt;/em&gt; for expected movements. This fits within predictive coding and active inference theories, where the brain continually predicts sensory and motor consequences to optimize performance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In short (plain language)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This study shows that when people are told &lt;em&gt;what movement to prepare&lt;/em&gt;, they react faster if the cues are correct. This means &lt;strong&gt;attention and motor planning work together&lt;/strong&gt; to make actions quicker and more efficient, supporting theories that action preparation influences how fast we respond.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Why this is relevant for your experiment&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Although this study does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; directly measure &lt;em&gt;sense of agency&lt;/em&gt;, it demonstrates that:&lt;br&gt;✔ Motor preparation speeds reaction time&lt;br&gt;✔ Attention is involved when planning voluntary movement&lt;br&gt;✔ Valid motor predictions improve performance (faster RT)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This complements your results showing that &lt;strong&gt;active conditions&lt;/strong&gt; (with an action to execute) are behaviorally different (often faster RTs) compared to passive control conditions, because active trials engage &lt;strong&gt;motor prediction and attention mechanisms&lt;/strong&gt; that reduce reaction times.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you want, I can also find &lt;strong&gt;agency studies that explicitly link agency judgments with RT facilitation effects&lt;/strong&gt;. Just let me know!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
